## Core Concepts

### 1. Tables and Columns

```
Table Structure:
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│      CUSTOMER TABLE                 │
├─────────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┤
│CUST_ID  │ NAME │PHONE │EMAIL │CITY  │
├─────────┼──────┼──────┼──────┼──────┤
│ 100001  │ John │555-01│j@..  │NYC   │
│ 100002  │ Jane │555-02│j@..  │LA    │
│ 100003  │ Bob  │555-03│b@..  │CHI   │
└─────────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┘
```

### 2. Keys and Relationships

```
Primary Key:
├─ Unique identifier for row
├─ CUST_ID in CUSTOMER table
└─ Enforces uniqueness

Foreign Key:
├─ References another table's primary key
├─ ORDER.CUST_ID → CUSTOMER.CUST_ID
└─ Maintains referential integrity

Index:
├─ Sorted structure for fast lookup
├─ KEY: CUST_ID
└─ Reduces I/O operations
```

### 3. ACID Properties in DB2

```
Atomicity:
├─ INSERT & UPDATE both succeed or both fail
└─ Never partial transactions

Consistency:
├─ All constraints enforced
├─ Data always valid
└─ Referential integrity maintained

Isolation:
├─ Concurrent transactions isolated
├─ No dirty reads
├─ Repeatable reads guaranteed
└─ Phantom reads prevented

Durability:
├─ Committed data never lost
├─ Survives any failure
├─ Logged before commit
└─ Recoverable from disk
```

### 4. Transactions

```
Transaction Lifecycle:

BEGIN
  │
  ├─ SQL Statement 1 (INSERT)
  ├─ SQL Statement 2 (UPDATE)
  ├─ SQL Statement 3 (DELETE)
  │
  ├─ COMMIT (success path)
  │  └─ All changes written
  │  └─ Lock released
  │  └─ Other users see changes
  │
  └─ ROLLBACK (failure path)
     └─ All changes undone
     └─ Database unchanged
     └─ Lock released
```

