## Backup & Recovery

### Theory: Recovery Levels

```
Recovery Objectives:

RTO (Recovery Time Objective):
├─ How long to restore service
├─ Banking: < 1 hour
├─ Critical apps: < 4 hours
├─ Non-critical: < 24 hours

RPO (Recovery Point Objective):
├─ How much data loss acceptable
├─ Banking: < 1 minute
├─ Critical apps: < 1 hour
├─ Non-critical: < 1 day

Technology Enables Different Levels:
├─ Synchronous replication: RPO = 0
├─ Asynchronous replication: RPO = 1 minute
├─ Daily backups: RPO = 1 day
└─ Weekly backups: RPO = 1 week
```

### Backup Strategy

```
Incremental Approach:

Level 0 (Full Backup):
├─ Every Sunday 1 AM
├─ All tables, indexes
├─ Time: 2 hours
├─ Size: 500GB
└─ Frequency: Weekly

Level 1 (Cumulative Incremental):
├─ Every Wednesday 1 AM
├─ Changes since Level 0
├─ Time: 30 minutes
├─ Size: 50GB
└─ Frequency: Weekly

Level 2 (Differential):
├─ Every day (Mon-Sat) 2 AM
├─ Changes since Level 1
├─ Time: 10 minutes
├─ Size: 10GB
└─ Frequency: Daily

Recovery Path:
└─ Level 0 + Level 1 + Level 2 (since last Level 1)
└─ Restore time: 2 + 0.5 + 0.2 hours = ~2.5 hours
└─ Complete recovery with all recent changes
```

### Point-in-Time Recovery

```
Transaction Log:

All changes recorded:
├─ INSERT statements
├─ UPDATE statements  
├─ DELETE statements
├─ Timestamps
└─ Used for recovery

Recovery Process:

Scenario: Delete statement mistakenly ran 3:45 PM

Steps:
1. Restore from latest backup (2:00 PM)
   └─ Database state at 2:00 PM

2. Roll forward using transaction log
   └─ Read all transactions 2:00 PM to 3:44 PM
   └─ Apply all changes
   └─ Database now at 3:44 PM

3. Stop recovery (just before delete)
   └─ Skip the problematic delete statement
   └─ Database state restored correctly

Result:
└─ Full recovery with minimal data loss
└─ Only 1 minute of transactions lost
└─ All other users' changes preserved
```

